// The Mach half of the fake kernel: traps, port names, and the kernel's side
// of Mach messages.
//
// On Mac OS X most kernel services are not system calls but messages sent to
// kernel-owned ports (the task port, the host port, a clock...). The message
// formats are generated by MIG from interface definitions; each request is a
// header, a small "NDR" record, then the arguments, and each reply mirrors it
// with a return code. kernel_rpc() answers the ones programs really use.
//
// The ports themselves are real: every name the guest holds is a port of this
// host process, under the same name. Ports the guest makes for itself, the
// rights it is sent, port sets and semaphores are simply the host's, and the
// calls that manage them are passed on to the host kernel. Only the kernel
// objects (task, thread, host, clock) are cocaine's own: each is a port this
// process holds the receiving end of, and a message the guest sends to one is
// answered here instead of travelling anywhere.

